{# Safety net, not a real template.

   Grav picks a template from the page's filename: default.md asks for
   default.html.twig, home.md asks for home.html.twig. A theme that provides
   only home.html.twig therefore hard-errors on any page named default.md —
   which is exactly what Grav's stock install ships, and exactly how this theme
   failed on first activation:

     ERROR: default.html.twig template not found for page: /

   Rather than leave that landmine for whoever adds a second page, anything
   asking for `default` renders through the same layout. It will show an empty
   body, since home.html.twig owns the actual sections — but an empty styled
   page is a far better failure than a stack trace, and it makes the cause
   obvious instead of fatal. #}
{% extends 'partials/base.html.twig' %}

{% block body %}
  <main class="section">
    <div class="container">
      {{ page.content|raw }}
    </div>
  </main>
{% endblock %}
